I must admit that Strife is the first Action Real Time Tactics after DotA that I really enjoy playing. I more or less played all the other games of this genre that were released in the last ten years, included Demigod (first commercial game of its kind), Minions (a browser based game), Smite (since beta) and Awesomenauts (bought with an Humble Bundle) and soon I got bored of each of them (except Heroes of Newerth, but when I played it it was still in beta and too much similar to DotA to make me to want to pay for it). I find instead Strife very entertaining, good to intersperse it with DotA/Dota 2 when you just want less demanding matches (like I did with the 3vs3 map on League of Legends). Heroes of the Storm too is funny, but in my opinion it's too much different from a classical ARTT to be in direct competition with the other games on the market.
The gameplay of Strife is a mix of features of DotA/HoN and LoL, while the graphics is cartoonish like LoL but with more details and a far better graphical engine. Strife runs on the Kodiak engine made by S2 Games, an evolution of the K2 engine used for Heroes of Newerth, a pretty solid graphics renderer (in my opinion even better than Source) and very light to run on relatively low performance computers.
The gameplay of Strife is a lot less stressful and with faster matches. There's less heroes in Strife than any other major Action RTT on the market, but at the moment I think they are more interesting (gameplay-wise) than most of the characters you can play on other games, probably because they are more refined. It's nothing really new in the field (Heroes of the Storm has much more innovations) but it's already a game more fun to play than League of Legends.
The gameplay elements I liked are:
- turn rates and casting animations. Those help a lot to the readability of the action;
- no masteries and rune system. Every hero starts at the same level. Crafting could be seen as an alternative rune system, but since it changes also the item cost in-game this makes it much more balanced;
- pet abilities aren't game breaking like summoner's spells in League of Legends (I think of spells like Smite, Ignite and Flash);
- the courier. You don't have to return in base just to get the items you bought in lane;
- more focus into pushing than just killing heroes. On League of Legends pushing is so devalued that often teams doesn't bring down turrets just to extend the lane phase, and that's ridiculous for a game that has destroying the enemy base as an objective;
- almost nearly all the heroes of Smite can fit many playstyles without the necessity to follow a fixed build. Yes, there's a bunch of supports out there, but S2 Games wants that supports too can be playable in offensive style;
- no mandatory jungler in a team. Killing neutral creeps is less important than achieving map objectives like Baldir and Cindara that can help a team to win the entire match, not only an opportunity to farm.
- all heroes are completely free like in Dota and HoN and you don't need to unlock them with real currency or in-game currency like in League of Legends or Heroes of the Storm. This encourages everyone to change often heroes and not to play just two or three of them.
In conclusion, Strife it's a well made Action RTT made to be less stressful than DotA/Dota 2 and LoL, and I recommend it to anyone who wants to take a break from more challenging games of the same kind.

